<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Proto-kingdom of Thinis</h1><p class="st-meta">3300 BCE – 3100 BCE (approx.) · capital Thinis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Thinis and Abydos, whose Dynasty 0 rulers left the earliest Egyptian writing and went on to unite Egypt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thinis">Thinis</a>, near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, became the seat of a line of rulers in the late 4th millennium BCE. Their burials in Cemetery U at Abydos grew steadily richer, and by c. 3200 BCE the Thinite chiefs had absorbed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/proto-kingdom-of-naqada">Proto-kingdom of Naqada</a> and extended their reach over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/proto-kingdom-of-nekhen">Proto-kingdom of Nekhen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomb-u-j">Tomb U-j</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a>, built around 3320 BCE, contained hundreds of imported Levantine wine jars and small bone labels carrying some of the earliest known Egyptian writing. Kings now wrote their names inside a serekh, a palace façade topped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> falcon. These rulers, known today as Dynasty 0, ran an economy of grain, wine and prestige goods and pushed their trade into the Delta.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Thinite line culminated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a>, who around 3100 BCE ruled both Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and is usually counted as the founder of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abydos">Abydos</a> remained the burial ground of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> kings and later became the great cult centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/proto-kingdom-of-thinis.jpg" alt="Proto-kingdom of Thinis" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous 4th millenium BCE scibe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Name_of_King_Iry-Hor,_Dynasty_0,_Eastern_Kom,_Tell_el-Farkha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>3300 BCE – 3100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd>34th century BCE – 32nd century BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thinis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Iry-Hor, Ka, Narmer</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dynasty 0, Thinite kingdom, Kingdom of Abydos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-3200&amp;focus=proto-kingdom-of-thinis" aria-label="Proto-kingdom of Thinis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 1211 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Proto-kingdom of Thinis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5842 1467L5869 1510M5842 1467L5813 1344"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5869 1510h0M5813 1344h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5842 1467h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5842 1467h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Thinis. <a href="/atlas?year=-3200&amp;focus=proto-kingdom-of-thinis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
